PostgreSQL - Aggregate Functions
1. What is an Aggregate Function?: An aggregate function performs a calculation on multiple rows and returns one result.
For example, if we have 5 students, we can calculate:
How many students?
What is the average age?
What is the highest age?
What is the total of some numbers?
Example:SELECT COUNT(*) FROM students;

#commom function are:
i. COUNT()-->Counts the number of rows.
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM students;
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM students;
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT faculty) FROM students; --> Counts different faculties. distinct mens cant calculate duplicate or remove duplicate

ii. SUM()-->Calculates the total of a numeric column.
select sum(salary) from employess;

iii. avg()---> Calculates the average.
select avg(salary) from employess;

iv. max()-->Returns the highest value.
SELECT MAX(marks) FROM results;

v.min()--> returns the lowest value.
SELECT MAX(marks) FROM results;


2. what is GROUP BY?
Aggregate functions become especially useful with GROUP BY. Suppose our students table contains:
name   | faculty
-------+-----------
Ram    | Science
Sita   | Science
Hari   | Management
Gita   | Management
John   | Management

To count students in each faculty:
SELECT faculty, COUNT(*) FROM students GROUP BY faculty;
Result:
faculty    | count
-----------+------
Science    | 2
Management | 3

Average marks by subject
SELECT subject, AVG(marks) FROM results GROUP BY subject;